.TH SOL\-TRON\-TX 1 2026-10-25 "blocktools 0.3.1" "blocktools manual"
.SH NAME
sol\-tron\-tx \- build unsigned, decode and verify Solana and Tron transfers. The chain is detected
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
sol\-tron\-tx decode|verify|build [\-\-chain sol|tron] [\-\-expect JSON] [\-\-plan FILE] [HEX_OR_BASE64]
  decode           decode a transaction (hex or base64, argument or stdin): Solana wire tx (legacy/v0) or Tron raw_data / Transaction
  verify \-\-expect  Solana: {owner?, to?, lamports?, mint?, amount?, allow_programs?}
                   Tron:   {owner?, to?, amount_sun?, contract?, amount?, max_fee_limit_sun?, now_ms?}
                   (one TRX transfer or TRC\-20 transfer; signatures recovered and must be the owner)
  build \-\-plan F   unsigned transfer from a plan file (solBuildTransfer / tronBuildTransfer JSON, EXPORTS.md); prints hex
Takes no keys and signs nothing.
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
from the input (transaction bytes, or the plan's `from` address); `\-\-chain sol|tron` overrides.
Public data only: takes no keys and signs nothing.
.nf
.RS 2
  sol\-tron\-tx decode < tx.hex
  sol\-tron\-tx verify \-\-expect '{"to":"...","lamports":"1000"}' < tx.hex
  sol\-tron\-tx build \-\-plan plan.json
.RE
.fi
.SH OPTIONS
.nf
  \-\-chain sol|tron skip auto\-detect. Without it, bytes that parse as both chains, or neither, are refused (bad_input).
.fi
.SH INPUT/OUTPUT
from the input (transaction bytes, or the plan's `from` address); `\-\-chain sol|tron` overrides.
.nf
.RS 2
  sol\-tron\-tx build \-\-plan plan.json
.RE
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
Measured on Linux by the EXAMPLES below (scripts/gen\-man.py runs them):
.TP
0
last status ok
.TP
2
last status bad_input
.PP
Not every tool uses the same code for bad input: some exit 1, others exit 2 (docs/FINDINGS.md D1\-14). Always read the last JSON status line. Status and failure codes: docs/REFUSALS.md.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
$ sol\-tron\-tx build \-\-plan "$R/recipes/fixtures/sol\-tron/sol\-plan.json"
# exit 0, last status: ok
$ sol\-tron\-tx decode < "$R/recipes/fixtures/sol\-tron/tron\-transfer.txt"
# exit 0, last status: ok
$ sol\-tron\-tx verify \-\-expect '{"lamports":"1000"}' < "$R/recipes/fixtures/sol\-tron/sol\-transfer.txt"
# exit 0, last status: ok
$ sol\-tron\-tx decode ff00ff
# exit 2, last status: bad_input
$ sol\-tron\-tx verify \-\-expect '{"to":"TT8jJMVHtAjgGFrc463ZwZQnMXEr519q3u","amount":"100000000000"}' < "$R/recipes/fixtures/sol\-tron/tron\-mainnet\-trc20.txt"
# exit 0, last status: ok
.fi
.SH SECURITY NOTES
DANGEROUS: use at your own risk. Code that handles keys and signs transactions should always be read and audited by you before you trust it with real funds. Used incorrectly, it can destroy your money.
Used incorrectly (wrong address, huge fee, lost keys, wrong chain) you lose funds; verify every transaction.
It may open hostile wallet files and handle secrets: run it sealed and offline (./blocktools run; sealed\-run allows it).
Unit tests are evidence, not a substitute for your own review. See docs/SECURITY.md.
